@startuml flow-daily-digest
' One digest run, morning or evening.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-daily-digest.puml

' Explicit near-white, NOT transparent: the text is dark, and a transparent background
' renders black on a dark viewer, which makes the whole diagram unreadable.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FDFDFD
  BorderColor #5B5BD6
  ArrowColor #59606F
  DiamondBackgroundColor #EFF1F6
  DiamondBorderColor #5B5BD6
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #C2670A
}

title Daily practice digest — 09:00 and 18:15

start

:scheduler fires (app.mail.zone);

if (MAIL_ENABLED?) then (no)
  :log and stop;
  stop
endif

if (mail transport configured?) then (no)
  #FFE0E0:log at ERROR and stop;
  note right
    Loud on purpose. The alternative is a job that
    appears to run every morning and delivers
    nothing at all.
  end note
  stop
endif

:one query for every subscriber
(DigestRepository.subscribers);

note right
  One query, not a loop. Rank, this week's solves
  and the revision queue all arrive with the row —
  four round trips per person against a cold Neon
  instance is the difference between a run that
  finishes and one still going at breakfast.

  Ordered most-recently-active first: that is what
  the budget cuts against.
end note

if (which run?) then (EVENING)
  :drop anyone who has already practised today;
  note right
    The reason there is a second send rather than a
    repeat. "You have already done your practice" at
    6pm is noise, and two near-identical emails a day
    is how people learn to ignore both.
  end note
else (MORNING)
  :everyone subscribed;
endif

:budget = MailQuotaService.remainingToday();

if (recipients > budget?) then (yes)
  #FFE0E0:truncate, and log exactly how many were skipped;
  note right
    Announced, never silent. A job that quietly mails
    half its users looks identical to one that mailed
    all of them.

    The budget is for the whole DAY across both runs,
    and is shared with sign-in codes — overspending it
    stops people signing in.
  end note
endif

repeat :for each recipient;
  :classify the situation from lastActiveDate
  (streak alive / at risk / lapsed / dormant / new);
  :DigestNarrator writes ONE line;

  if (model available and usable reply?) then (yes)
    :use it;
  else (no)
    :use the written fallback for
    that situation and run;
    note right
      Rate limit, cold start, markdown, a preamble,
      no API key — all the same here. Nothing about a
      language model may stop the mail.
    end note
  endif

  :render subject and body;
  note right
    Every **number** comes from the database row.
    The model is forbidden from quoting statistics:
    congratulating somebody on a streak they do not
    have costs more trust than a plain template.

    Greeting and subject follow the run, not the clock.
  end note

  :BrevoMailClient.send() — HTTP;
repeat while (more recipients?) is (yes)
->no;

:MailQuotaService.record(sent)
in its own transaction;
note right
  These messages cannot be un-sent, so forgetting
  them because the run failed later would let the
  next run spend the same budget again.
end note

:log sent / failed / skipped;

stop

@enduml
